<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scheduled Castes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Scheduled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Castes</a> is the official term in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> for the communities historically subjected to untouchability, listed in a schedule under the constitution. The constitution of 1950, drafted under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a>, abolished untouchability and reserved seats in legislatures and places in education and public employment for them. They make up about a sixth of India's population, and many use the self-chosen name Dalit.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes.jpg" alt="Scheduled Castes" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M Tracy Hunter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2011_Census_Scheduled_Caste_caste_distribution_map_India_by_state_and_union_territory.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
